+## Strategy & Approach
+
+- **Overall approach chosen**: Structural classification based on gate inputs (not output names), then check if actual output name matches expected naming convention for that role.
+- **Why this approach**: Output names are swapped but input structure is preserved. Classification by inputs is robust to output name changes.
+- **Key insights**:
+  1. Bit-0 half-adder: XOR(x00,y00)→z00 (not t00!), AND(x00,y00)→c00
+  2. For bit>0: XOR(xN,yN)→tNN (partial sum, internal), XOR(tNN,c(N-1))→zNN (final sum)
+  3. Swapped wire pairs always involve one z-prefix and one non-z-prefix name (in standard naming)
+  4. Circular dependencies arise when a swapped output name becomes an input to the same gate
+- **Assumptions made**:
+  - All circuits are acyclic (problem statement guarantees this)
+  - The naming convention follows the pattern described (z##, t##, a##, b##, c## for internal wires)
+  - Random names only apply to non-x/y/z wires
+- **Blockers/risks identified**:
+  1. **Cycle problem**: When swapping output names, the gate-graph dependency propagation can create cycles (a gate's output wire name appears as its own transitive input). This prevents x-dependency computation for affected gates.
+  2. **Random name problem**: Bit-position determination for derived gates currently relies on wire naming patterns. With random names, this fails entirely.
+  3. Need a name-independent way to determine gate bit position — possibly by analyzing the gate's position in the circuit topology (distance from inputs, or which x-wires it logically depends on through the gate structure).
